---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const rounds = [
  {
    name: 'Havdalah',
    ref: 'Vayikra 10:10 · O.C. 299:10',
    verse: 'וּלְהַבְדִּיל בֵּין הַקֹּדֶשׁ וּבֵין הַחֹל',
    verseEn: '“…to distinguish between the holy and between the mundane”',
    mechaber: 'הַמַּבְדִּיל בֵּין הַקֹּדֶשׁ וּבֵין הַחֹל',
    mechaberEn: 'Two “bein”s, exactly like the verse',
    rema: 'הַמַּבְדִּיל בֵּין קֹדֶשׁ לְחֹל',
    remaEn: 'One “bein” + “le-”, the Sages’ idiom',
  },
  {
    name: 'Challah',
    ref: 'Bamidbar 15:20 · Y.D. 328:1',
    verse: 'רֵאשִׁית עֲרִסֹתֵכֶם חַלָּה תָּרִימוּ תְרוּמָה',
    verseEn: '“The first of your dough, a loaf, you shall set apart as a terumah”',
    mechaber: 'לְהַפְרִישׁ תְּרוּמָה',
    mechaberEn: '“to separate terumah”, the Torah’s term',
    rema: 'לְהַפְרִישׁ חַלָּה',
    remaEn: '“to separate challah”, the Mishnah’s term',
  },
  {
    name: 'Shemini Atzeret',
    ref: 'Vayikra 23:36 · O.C. 668:1',
    verse: 'בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי מִקְרָא קֹדֶשׁ יִהְיֶה לָכֶם … עֲצֶרֶת הִוא',
    verseEn: '“On the eighth day there shall be a holy convocation for you … it is an atzeret”',
    mechaber: 'יוֹם שְׁמִינִי חַג הָעֲצֶרֶת הַזֶּה',
    mechaberEn: 'The name is “Shemini”; atzeret describes it',
    rema: 'יוֹם שְׁמִינִי עֲצֶרֶת הַזֶּה',
    remaEn: 'The name is “Shemini Atzeret”, Chazal’s title',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="The Language of Blessing: Rav Yaakov Kamenetsky on Biblical vs. Rabbinic Hebrew"
  description="An animated explainer of Rav Yaakov Kamenetsky's Emet LeYaakov on why the Mechaber and the Rema word Havdalah, Challah and Shemini Atzeret differently: Torah language vs. the language of the Sages."
>
  <main class="container mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 sm:py-16">
    <article class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <header class="mb-10 text-center not-prose">
        <p class="text-sm tracking-[0.3em] uppercase text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Rav Yaakov Kamenetsky · Emet LeYaakov</p>
        <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-text-light dark:text-text-dark sm:text-5xl">
          The Language of Blessing
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-2xl" dir="rtl" lang="he">לְשׁוֹן הַמִּקְרָא · לְשׁוֹן חֲכָמִים</p>
      </header>

      <div class="not-prose mb-12">
        <video controls preload="metadata" poster="/videos/language-of-blessing-poster.jpg" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-2xl">
          <source src="/videos/language-of-blessing.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          Your browser does not support the video tag.
        </video>
        <p class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-gray-500">4:36 · 1080p · Erev Shemini Atzeret 5787</p>
      </div>

      <h2>The question</h2>
      <p>
        Hebrew comes in two registers: <strong>leshon hamikra</strong>, the Torah's own words, and
        <strong>leshon chachamim</strong>, the idiom of the Mishnah and the Sages. Chazal composed our
        blessings, but whose Hebrew did they use? Rav Yaakov notices that the Shulchan Aruch (the
        Mechaber, R. Yosef Karo) and the Rema (R. Moshe Isserles) disagree on the wording of three
        blessings, and in each case the split is the same.
      </p>

      {rounds.map((r, i) => (
        <section class="not-prose my-10 rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-6">
          <p class="text-xs tracking-[0.25em] uppercase text-gray-500">Round {i + 1} · {r.ref}</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 text-2xl font-bold">{r.name}</h3>
          <p class="mt-4 text-2xl text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-300" dir="rtl" lang="he">{r.verse}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 italic text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">{r.verseEn}</p>
          <div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 p-4">
              <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-300">Mechaber · Torah's phrasing</p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-xl" dir="rtl" lang="he">{r.mechaber}</p>
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">{r.mechaberEn}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-cyan-50 dark:bg-cyan-950/40 p-4">
              <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-cyan-700 dark:text-cyan-300">Rema · Sages' phrasing</p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-xl" dir="rtl" lang="he">{r.rema}</p>
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">{r.remaEn}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
      ))}

      <h2>Theory one: a real dispute about style</h2>
      <blockquote dir="rtl" lang="he">ונראה שפליגי אי חז״ל תקנו הברכות לפי לשון התורה או לפי לשון חכמים</blockquote>
      <p>
        <em>“It seems they disagree whether Chazal instituted the blessings according to the language of
        the Torah or the language of the Sages.”</em> The Vilna Gaon already read the challah dispute this
        way: <span dir="rtl" lang="he">באיזו סגנון צריך לברך, אם בלשון המקרא או בלשון המשנה</span>.
        On this reading the Mechaber and the Rema are each consistent (<span dir="rtl" lang="he">אזלי לשיטתייהו</span>):
        the Mechaber picks Torah language every time and the Rema picks the Sages' language every time.
      </p>

      <h2>Theory two: they don't disagree at all</h2>
      <blockquote dir="rtl" lang="he">דאם המצוה היא דאורייתא תקנו ברכתה בלשון המקרא, ואם קיום המצוה אינה אלא מדרבנן גם נוסחת הברכה בלשון חכמים תקנוה</blockquote>
      <p>
        <em>“If the mitzvah is from the Torah, they instituted its blessing in the language of Scripture;
        if fulfilling the mitzvah is only rabbinic, they also instituted the blessing's text in the
        language of the Sages.”</em> Both agree on the rule. What differs is the mitzvah's status:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Challah:</strong> the Mechaber lived in Eretz Yisrael, where challah is a Torah
          obligation, so he writes <span dir="rtl" lang="he">להפריש תרומה</span>. The Rema lived in Poland,
          where challah is rabbinic, so he writes <span dir="rtl" lang="he">להפריש חלה</span>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Havdalah:</strong> the Rambam holds havdalah is <em>d'oraita</em>, like Kiddush. The Mechaber
          rules <span dir="rtl" lang="he">נשים חייבות בהבדלה כשם שחייבות בקידוש</span>, so for him it is from the Torah:
          <span dir="rtl" lang="he">בין הקודש ובין החול</span>. The Rema rules <span dir="rtl" lang="he">לא יבדילו לעצמן</span>,
          worrying that it is only rabbinic, so he writes <span dir="rtl" lang="he">בין קודש לחול</span>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Shemini Atzeret:</strong> in Eretz Yisrael it is one day and always from the Torah, so the
          Mechaber uses the Torah's name. Outside the Land there are two days, and the second, Simchat Torah, is
          rabbinic. If the Rema said “Yom Shemini” on day one and “Shemini Atzeret” on day two,
          <span dir="rtl" lang="he">אתי לזלזולי ביום טוב של שמחת תורה</span>: the different wording would lead
          people to belittle Simchat Torah. So he says <span dir="rtl" lang="he">יום שמיני עצרת</span> on both days.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
        Source: Emet LeYaakov (new edition), Vayikra, Parashat Shemini (on 10:10) and Parashat Emor (on 23:36),
        pp. 443–444, with Shulchan Aruch O.C. 296, 299, 668 and Y.D. 328.
      </p>
      <p class="text-center text-2xl" dir="rtl" lang="he">חַג שָׂמֵחַ · וְדוֹ״ק</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
